# Is Burning a Crime?

From the day the world appeared, civilised people have destroyed what was unnecessary and harmful to their lives, produced what they needed, and begun to live in a civilised manner. At first, people cleared forests, that is, burned them, and created beautiful settlements along riverbanks. The historical record of nations also explains that, after people learned how to make fire, they used it both to cook the food they required and to destroy what they did not need. A brief examination of the three periods, the puranic age, the historical age, and the present, makes it clear that people did not regard burning unwanted things as a crime.

Everyone knows the story of Lord Siva, described as the Supreme God, burning the three cities. It is said that this is how he acquired the special name "the matted-haired god who burned Tripura." In the *Mahabharata* we find the account of Arjuna burning the Khandava forest. In the *Ramayana* we may read of Hanuman burning Lanka. Burning dead bodies and heaps of refuse has been an ancient practice from the earliest times.

We know that Mamallan, the Pallava king who ruled Tamil Nadu in the seventh century, defeated his opponent Pulikesi, the Chalukya king, and burned his capital Vatapi. Nor have people forgotten the burning in August 1942 of transport systems that were like the country's nerves and of many other essential buildings. In August, they burned not only unnecessary things but necessary ones too. Even that became a sacrifice in this country.

But the seductive, poison-coated sword called the *Kamba Ramayanam* lies in the hand of the enemy called Aryan religion, which disrupts the distinctive life of the Tamil people. We too appreciate the poetic grace and poetic sensibility of the *Kamba Ramayanam*; we reject only its ideas. We know that burning one copy cannot abolish the *Kamba Ramayanam* from the country. Yet we say it should be burned to demonstrate our opposition to it. When we say this, scholars tremble.

Theists!

It is you who believe in God and divine power. You know the saying that those who trust God will not be forsaken. You tell stories that the three-eyed Lord saved Appar from the lime kiln and Krishna saved Prahlada from blazing fire. As proof that Tamil possesses divinity, you say Sambandar sent a palm-leaf manuscript upstream on the Vaigai. You believe it will not be swept away by flood or consumed by blazing flame, and you also believe in the divine power of the *Kamba Ramayanam*. If so, why are you afraid of this? Pray for the grace of the matted-haired god who burned Tripura. Ask him to stop the burning of his companion Kambar's *Ramayanam*. Let the *Kamba Ramayanam* remain in the country as art after its ideas have been scorched away.

Rational people!

As examples of people burning what they do not want, you have seen the story of the trident-bearing god burning Tripura, the puranic story of Hanuman setting Lanka afire, Mamallan burning Vatapi, and the Indian National Congress burning foreign cloth. Tell us: is it only the burning of the *Kamba Ramayanam*, which conflicts with reason and undermines Tamil self-respect, that is a crime?

30-12-1945
